#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537998} :activity/id #uuid "e3578813-12a2-53c7-b590-c0aff695b404" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048423 :topic/id #uuid "9ddcc703-a984-5097-a142-236dc3c2fbaf" :topic/math-academy-id 2979} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537998 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537914 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "13c010e0-d904-51ec-b491-72fa13a1c722" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537265} {:answer/id #uuid "1ab45d96-d124-5fb4-b8cc-95b11dfddc6c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537283} {:answer/id #uuid "7e6209ff-c890-5b77-aea9-3b062a5bffb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537476} {:answer/id #uuid "b0fe6e5e-9156-5eb2-886b-f6d9bd66017f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537569} {:answer/id #uuid "c2efe1d1-723f-56a8-b37f-a809b02fb084" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537603}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537265} :answer-field/id #uuid "000395f2-2229-5afc-a0c3-6b50cd88d3b5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537757}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "543d7533-dccc-5fe7-9798-fd9513ac4953" :question/math-academy-id "q-88456" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation $2{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{x+2}-32\\cdot {5}^{x+2}=0?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{x+2}-32\\cdot {5}^{x+2} & =0 \\\\ (2{x}^{2}-32)\\cdot {5}^{x+2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}-32=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{5}^{x+2}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $2{x}^{2}-32=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2({x}^{2}-16) & =0 \\\\ 2(x+4)(x-4) & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-4,4.$\n\n- The equation $2=0$ is not a possible solution because it's a false statement.\n\n- The equation ${5}^{x+2}=0$ has no solutions because ${5}^{x+2}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-4,4,$ and their product is $(-4)\\cdot 4=-16.$"} :step/id #uuid "1e124127-bb29-56d9-aea9-e096e5bceea6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538025}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538025 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186354646 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33737a4b-5342-5565-914a-cb6438076b5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352043} {:answer/id #uuid "380810d5-4536-54a8-96a6-03ca61571cf7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352085} {:answer/id #uuid "4d84059a-bf3e-5ad8-848d-de47fb5fcbba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352275} {:answer/id #uuid "8597e99b-a624-5fb1-9f86-e86812b0613d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186352731} {:answer/id #uuid "da180e83-5bee-502b-9c12-ae8aed810433" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353461}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186353461} :answer-field/id #uuid "15a5b309-fce0-58d4-9cb6-f4a8f8750bae" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186353852}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "395668d0-2b32-5884-a30f-d67a196224fd" :question/math-academy-id "q-88460" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation $2{x}^{2}\\cdot {6}^{x+3}-9x\\cdot {6}^{x+3}+4\\cdot {6}^{x+3}=0?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}\\cdot {6}^{x+3}-9x\\cdot {6}^{x+3}+4\\cdot {6}^{x+3} & =0 \\\\ (2{x}^{2}-9x+4){6}^{x+3} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n2{x}^{2}-9x+4=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{6}^{x+3}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $2{x}^{2}-9x+4=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(2x-1)(x-4)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=\\frac{1}{2},4.$\n\n- The equation ${6}^{x+3}=0$ has no solutions because ${6}^{x+3}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=\\frac{1}{2},4,$ and their product is $\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 4=2.$"} :step/id #uuid "698684ba-c1ee-5c3c-b7f8-006e905d879a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538045}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186538045 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537962 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "513cdd03-c241-5b39-91b6-0279ee268055" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537398} {:answer/id #uuid "70cc8f24-5b90-57ac-b62e-1362fb9e8618" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537449} {:answer/id #uuid "76c93d58-5244-5182-bfad-36ccd0a40c29" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537465} {:answer/id #uuid "949f3a69-bede-5a74-a0b5-be25455f3451" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537508} {:answer/id #uuid "df115754-c961-559d-9269-701572e5321c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537677}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186537677} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e621ebc-06c8-5c50-ae08-59bcabc99cdf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537773}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "bb3bd545-e795-5869-ab36-2a8ac7b0ac72" :question/math-academy-id "q-88461" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation $4\\cdot {5}^{x-2}={x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{x-2}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\cdot {5}^{x-2} & ={x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{x-2} \\\\ 4\\cdot {5}^{x-2}-{x}^{2}\\cdot {5}^{x-2} & =0 \\\\ (4-{x}^{2}){5}^{x-2} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n4-{x}^{2}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{5}^{x-2}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $4-{x}^{2}=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(2+x)(2-x)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-2,2.$\n\n- The equation ${5}^{x-2}=0$ has no solutions because ${5}^{x-2}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-2,2,$ and their product is $(-2)\\cdot 2=-4.$"} :step/id #uuid "ab84e546-33f9-5443-89fa-b2f73b6f13b0"}] :activity/title "Solving Exponential Equations Using the Zero-Product Property" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186537221}]}
